  "member_id": "linnarsson-sten",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/medicine_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B006",
        "laureates": [
          "Jacques F. A. P. Miller",
          "Max D. Cooper"
        ],
        "citation": "for discoveries of distinct B- and T-lymphocyte lineages that established the organization of adaptive immunity"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B012",
    "B021"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "I have changed my preferred configuration from B012 to B006. A narrowly framed award for the distinct B- and T-lymphocyte lineages recognizes a fundamental biological organization that has remained durable across decades. Miller's thymus-dependent lineage work and Cooper's complementary B-cell lineage work form a coherent, distinct two-person account. Relative to the other leading achievements, this configuration best combines conceptual reach, maturity, and a delimited attribution.",
    "case_against": "The early history of lymphocyte-lineage biology included other important contributors, so the citation must not imply credit for all cellular immunology or later applications in vaccination, immunotherapy, and clinical medicine. The achievement is historically remote, and B012 offers an exceptionally compelling disease-specific molecular explanation with direct medical consequences.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "perlmann-thomas",
        "point": "Perlmann argues that B006 is strongest because Miller and Cooper supplied complementary, distinguishable elements of the adaptive-immunity framework, while insisting that its historical boundary be carefully limited.",
        "response": "I agree with that delimitation. It resolves the principal concern about B006: the proposed citation attributes only the establishment of distinct B- and T-cell lineages, not the subsequent development of immunology. On that formulation, the paired credit is more exact than I initially judged."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "sandberg-rickard",
        "point": "Sandberg considers B012 exceptionally strong but notes that Collins and Tsui are especially associated with positional cloning while Riordan's integral contribution raises a difficult three-person allocation question.",
        "response": "This sharpened my reassessment of B012. Mapping, cloning, and molecular characterization were interdependent in identifying CFTR, but the supplied discussion does not provide a sufficiently precise basis for resolving how the three names represent the broad collaborative enterprise. That residual attribution uncertainty now favors the more delimited Miller-Cooper configuration."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "svenningsson-per",
        "point": "Svenningsson recognizes the exceptional clinical benefit of GLP-1 therapies but identifies unresolved credit across peptide biology, physiology, pharmacological optimization, and translation.",
        "response": "I agree that B021 is a major alternative on medical impact. Its broad trajectory nevertheless makes it difficult to define a single achievement and exact laureate allocation without absorbing later development. B006 more cleanly joins a foundational result to an appropriately bounded credit assignment."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether the historical record can delimit Miller's and Cooper's contributions from those of other early investigators with sufficient precision under the proposed citation.",
      "Whether a disease-specific causal discovery such as B012 should comparatively outweigh B006's broader biological organization despite B012's more compressed attribution."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile. My documented coauthorship relates to spatial transcriptomics and does not identify a connection to either proposed B006 laureate."
  }
}
